:: R5_HarperAfternoon [r5 story intimate]
<<location "Conference Room B Deck, Floor 39">>
<<portrait "files/images/mood/r5-harper-conference-tension.png" "Harper Quinn">>
<<objective "Finish the Waverly deck with Harper.">>

Harper and I work in the glass-walled conference room on B deck for three hours after lunch. She's building the analytics backbone for my Waverly presentation. I'm editing the narrative deck. We sit across from each other at the long table with laptops open and coffee going cold.

She's good at this. Better than me, honestly. She sees patterns in numbers the way some people see faces in clouds, except her faces are always actually there. She pulls a correlation between Waverly's customer churn rate and their marketing spend cadence that I would have missed entirely.

<<sayself>>You're genius at this.<</sayself>>

I mean it.

<<say "Harper Quinn">>I'm adequate at this.<</say>>

She smiles without looking up.

<<say "Harper Quinn">>You want genius, you pay genius rates. You want Harper Quinn, you get a junior analyst who reads too many statistics blogs and drinks too much coffee.<</say>>

<<sayself>>I'll put that in your performance review.<</sayself>>

<<say "Harper Quinn">>Please don't.<</say>>

We work. The afternoon light moves across the table. The building hums. This is the version of work I actually enjoy, the collaborative quiet, two people building something from data and argument and carefully chosen words. Harper smells like coffee and something clean, maybe vanilla, maybe just soap. She's got a freckle on her left wrist I've never noticed before.

She leans over my side of the table to point at a cell in my spreadsheet.

<<say "Harper Quinn">>This number's off. You've got the Q2 adjusted figure but the baseline is Q1 actual. Should be Q2 actual or the growth rate's inflated.<</say>>

Her arm extends across my space. Her forearm brushes my shoulder. Right over the bra strap.

The contact lasts maybe two seconds. Her arm against my shoulder, her skin warm through my shirt, and beneath the shirt the raised line of the strap, a quarter-inch ridge of fabric and elastic that shouldn't be there.

She pauses.

It's a small pause. A catch in the rhythm of her reach, a hesitation measured in fractions of a second. Her fingers still on the spreadsheet, her arm still extended, her forearm resting on my shoulder with the strap pressing into us both.

Then she withdraws. Sits back down. Looks at her screen.

<<say "Harper Quinn">>Fix the baseline. Everything else looks clean.<</say>>

Did she feel it. The question repeats in my skull like a stuck record. Did she feel it. Her forearm was right on it. The strap was right there. She paused. She definitely paused.

I fix the baseline. My hands are trembling. I type the number wrong twice before getting it right. Harper is looking at her screen and her expression reveals absolutely nothing, which is exactly the expression of someone who has catalogued something interesting and decided not to catalog it out loud.

We work for another hour. She doesn't lean over again. She doesn't touch my shoulder. She doesn't mention the pause. But once, when I look up, she's watching me with an expression I can't decode. Curiosity, maybe. Concern, maybe. Something else, maybe.

She looks away first.

<<npc "harper" "suspicion" 8>>
<<modstat "officeSuspicion" 3>>

